<h2>The prosecutions</h2>

<p>The petitions concerned complaints under the Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006 arising from samples of Maggi noodles collected in May 2015, at the height of a nationwide controversy. The Delhi Food Laboratory reported lead content in the masala tastemaker beyond the permissible limit of 2.5 parts per million, and one complaint additionally alleged misbranding in relation to the declaration concerning monosodium glutamate. On those reports, complaints were instituted invoking the Act's provisions on unsafe food and misbranding, and the trial court issued summoning orders on 6 November 2015 and 11 January 2016.</p>

<p>The petitioners — persons arrayed as being responsible for the conduct of the company's business — sought quashing of the complaints and all consequential proceedings.</p>

<h2>Why the foundation had eroded</h2>

<p>Justice Madhu Jain identified three developments, each the product of judicial scrutiny in the intervening decade, which together had hollowed out the prosecution case.</p>

<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><h3>The laboratory's status</h3><p>The state laboratory that furnished the founding reports lacked NABL accreditation and had not been notified as required under Section 43 of the FSSA at the time of testing. The Bombay High Court, in the connected litigation of 2015, had held that testing must come from a laboratory holding both credentials.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>The referral laboratory's findings</h3><p>Pursuant to the Supreme Court's direction in the civil proceedings, fresh testing was carried out at the Central Food Technological Research Institute, Mysuru — the statutory referral laboratory. Its reports found the lead content within permissible limits, contradicting the original analysis.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>The passage of scrutiny</h3><p>The controversy had by then travelled through the superior courts, and the scientific basis of the 2015 reports had not survived that examination. What remained was a prosecution resting on discredited documents.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The legal test applied</h2>

<p>The judgment proceeds on the settled principles governing the quashing of criminal proceedings: the categories illustrated in <em>State of Haryana v. Bhajan Lal</em>, 1992 Supp (1) SCC 335, and the approach in <em>Rajiv Thapar v. Madan Lal Kapoor</em>, (2013) 3 SCC 330, under which a High Court may act on material of sterling quality that would irresistibly lead to acquittal, so that continuation of the trial would be an abuse of the process of the court. The Court concluded that permitting the prosecutions to continue would serve no useful purpose, and quashed the complaints, the summoning orders and all consequential proceedings.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>The inherent power invoked in such petitions was found in Section 482 of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973 and is now located in Section 528 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023. The principles governing its exercise are unchanged: it is a power to prevent abuse of process and secure the ends of justice, exercised sparingly and not as a substitute for trial.</p>
</div>

<h2>What the case teaches about food safety prosecutions</h2>

<p>Prosecutions under the FSSA are distinctive in that the central witness is usually a document — the food analyst's report. The statute itself recognises the fallibility of a single analysis and builds in a corrective: the person from whom the sample is taken may require that the counterpart sample be analysed by a referral laboratory, whose report then supersedes the analyst's. Two consequences follow for practice.</p>

<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Point in time</th><th>What matters</th></tr>
  <tr><td>At sampling</td><td>Compliance with the statutory sampling procedure, preservation of counterpart samples, and the chain of custody. Defects here are frequently outcome-determinative years later.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>On receipt of the report</td><td>The accreditation and notification status of the testing laboratory, and timely exercise of the right to referral-laboratory analysis. Delay can forfeit the remedy because samples deteriorate.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>At the summoning stage</td><td>Whether the complaint is within limitation, whether the persons summoned are properly identified as responsible for the conduct of the business, and whether the founding report can lawfully support cognizance.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The broader principle</h2>

<p>The decision is not a special dispensation for a well-known brand; it is an application of a general principle with reach across regulatory prosecutions — drugs, weights and measures, environment, and food alike. Where the scientific document at the foundation of a criminal case is shown, through the statute's own corrective machinery or through judicial scrutiny, to be unreliable, the case does not limp on to an inevitable acquittal a decade hence. The High Court's inherent power exists precisely so that the process of the criminal court is not itself converted into a punishment.</p>

<p>For food business operators, the practical lesson lies in the early stages: the accreditation of the laboratory, the counterpart sample, and the referral remedy are not technicalities but the substance of the defence. For complainant authorities, the lesson is the mirror image — a prosecution launched on a report that cannot withstand the statute's own verification mechanism is a prosecution built on sand.</p>
